<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Königsberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1544">1544</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Albertina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, known as the Albertina, was founded in 1544 by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Prussia</a> as a Lutheran university. It became a leading centre of learning in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> studied and taught. It closed when the Soviet Army captured Königsberg in 1945; the city is now Kaliningrad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg.jpg" alt="University of Königsberg" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel-Albertina-Koenigsber.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Albert, former Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and first Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> since 1525, bought land behind Königsberg Cathedral on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kneiphof">Kneiphof</a> island in the Pregel River and had an academic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> built there in 1542. He issued the deed of foundation of the Collegium Albertinum on 20 July 1544, and the university was inaugurated on 17 August. It was the world's second Protestant academy, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>, and served as a Lutheran counterpart to the Catholic Kraków Academy. Its first rector was the poet Georg Sabinus, son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a>, and all professors swore an oath on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> withheld approval, but King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> granted the royal privilege on 28 March 1560.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The university first had four colleges, Theology, Medicine, Philosophy and Law, with natural sciences added later. The Prussian lands escaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, which increased its popularity with students. The first book in Lithuanian was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> in 1547, and the university had a deep effect on Lithuanian culture. Tsar Peter I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> visited in 1697, and Russian officials afterwards studied cameralist theory there. The 18th century, known as the Königsberg Century of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, brought Johann Georg Hamann and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Johann Gottfried Herder</a> among its alumni, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>, rector in 1786 and 1788.</p><p>A botanical garden opened in 1811, and Friedrich Wilhelm Bessel built his observatory beside it two years later. In the 19th and 20th centuries the university was best known for mathematics, in a school founded by Carl Gustav Jacob Jacobi and later associated with Hermann Minkowski, Adolf Hurwitz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hilbert">David Hilbert</a>. A new main building in neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style by Friedrich August Stüler was inaugurated in 1862, and in 1900 the university had 900 students.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The university marked its 400th anniversary on 10 July 1944. In August the city was heavily bombed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>'s offensive and the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> followed. When General Otto Lasch signed the capitulation on 9 April 1945, 80 per cent of the campus lay in ruins, and many of the faculty, who had fled, were received at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a> the city became Kaliningrad in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the Albertina was closed. In 1947 a new institution resumed work as the Kaliningrad State Pedagogical Institute, which became Kaliningrad State University in 1967. Today the Immanuel Kant Baltic Federal University claims to maintain the Albertina's traditions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Königsberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1544 Albert founded the <b>Albertina</b> University in Königsberg, the main school for the Lutheran pastors and theologians of Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Germany Gotthold Ephraim Lessing pleaded for tolerance among Jews, Christians and Muslims in his play Nathan the Wise of 1779, and Kant, who taught at the <b>University of Königsberg</b>, examined the limits of reason itself.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · People</a></li></ul></section>
